Ladybird sizes a popup by its document’s intrinsic width: It lays the
document out at 25×25, measures the root’s max-content inline size,
clamps it to 800, and lays the document out at that width for the
height, up to 600, again after each layout that changed something.
Gecko sizes an extension popup that way: ext-browser-content.js asks
nsDocumentViewer::GetContentSize(), which takes the root frame’s
GetPrefISize() clamped to the maximum width, reflows at that width with
ResizeReflow(), and reports the visible area. WebKit starts at 1×1 in
LocalFrameView::performSizeToContentAutoSize() and uses the document
renderer’s minContentLogicalWidthContribution() instead, so a popup
there is only as wide as its least-breakable content. Blink measures
neither: With AutoSizeUsesScrollWidthForOverflow shipped, FrameView-
AutoSizeInfo::AutoSizeIfNeeded() resets the frame to its minimum size
and reads the root’s scroll width, which can’t see the content that an
end-aligned root pushes past its start edge.

A popup closes itself with window.close() whatever its history holds.
Chromium does that for all of an extension’s pages, since
extension_webkit_preferences.cc sets allow_scripts_to_close_windows for
them, and ExtensionHost::CloseContents() then closes the host; Ladybird
does it for an extension’s popups, and not yet for its other pages.
